{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778780922,"nonce":"YIMJbItoC6nh4QHJO6l28pepsxs=","title":"Mandible plugin ecosystem and artifact store for research tool providers","message":"## Problem\n\nMandible is evolving from an auth/session dashboard into a local-first research control plane. It already tracks issues, branches, sessions, auth requests, work units, and graph views. The next cross-cutting step is to let specialized tools plug into Mandible as providers while Mandible owns project memory, artifact storage, provenance, issue/work graphs, and agent orchestration.\n\nFeedbax is the immediate motivating provider: a domain workbench for model/task/loss construction, training runs, evaluations, analyses, and deterministic reports. But the architecture should not be Feedbax-specific. Other tools should be able to register capabilities, schemas, UI actions, artifact types, and execution contracts in the same way.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nMandible should orchestrate and remember. Domain tools should execute and inspect.\n\nMandible owns:\n\n- Project/issue/work graphs.\n- Agent sessions and continuation context.\n- Auth/review gates.\n- Plugin/tool registry.\n- Artifact storage and metadata indexing.\n- Provenance edges between issues, sessions, branches, commits, specs, runs, reports, and artifacts.\n\nProviders own:\n\n- Domain-specific schemas.\n- Validation of specs.\n- Deterministic execution of runs/evaluations/analyses.\n- Domain UI entry points.\n- Domain-specific component registries and extension hooks.\n\n## Artifact Store\n\nMandible should provide a content-addressed artifact store for large outputs that should not be committed directly but must remain discoverable and linked to project memory.\n\nA minimal artifact record should include:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"artifact://sha256/\u003chash\u003e\",\n  \"sha256\": \"\u003chash\u003e\",\n  \"logical_name\": \"warmup_model.eqx\",\n  \"role\": \"training_checkpoint\",\n  \"media_type\": \"application/x-equinox\",\n  \"size_bytes\": 123456789,\n  \"producer_plugin\": \"feedbax\",\n  \"producer_entity\": \"training_run:\u003cid\u003e\",\n  \"storage_backend\": \"local\",\n  \"created_at\": \"...\",\n  \"metadata\": {}\n}\n```\n\nMVP backend can be local filesystem plus SQLite metadata. Later backends could include S3/R2, remote SSH volumes, git-annex/DVC-like storage, or project-specific stores. Providers should receive artifact IDs after upload/ingest and refer to those IDs in manifests instead of absolute paths.\n\n## Plugin/Provider Registry\n\nMandible should maintain a registry of installed providers. A provider declares:\n\n- Identity: name, version, project/repo, health check.\n- Capabilities: open, validate, execute, import/export, register component, report generation, etc.\n- Schemas: JSON Schema/OpenAPI or equivalent for accepted specs and emitted manifests.\n- Artifact roles and media types it can produce/consume.\n- UI actions and compatibility predicates.\n- Agent-facing tools/prompts/constraints.\n\nMandible should only show provider-specific actions when all are true:\n\n1. The provider is installed and healthy.\n2. The selected graph node/spec/artifact has a compatible type.\n3. The provider declares an action for that type.\n4. Permission/review policy allows the action.\n\nThis is what enables a button like \"Open in Feedbax Studio\" to appear only when Feedbax is available and the selected node is a Feedbax-compatible project/run/spec/artifact.\n\n## Agent Interaction\n\nAgents running inside Mandible should be able to query provider contracts:\n\n- What providers are installed?\n- Which provider can run this kind of experiment?\n- What schema must a training/evaluation/analysis spec satisfy?\n- What components/losses/tasks/analyses are available?\n- Can this generated spec be validated before execution?\n- If a new component is needed, what contract must it satisfy and where should it be registered?\n\nAgent-generated specs/components should still go through normal Mandible review/provenance. The provider contract gives agents bounded capabilities rather than making them guess hidden application internals.\n\n## Project Graph and Provenance\n\nMandible should distinguish two graph layers:\n\n1. Exhaustive provenance graph: auto-generated, noisy, truthful edges among issues, sessions, branches, commits, specs, manifests, artifacts, runs, reports.\n2. Curated project graph: human/agent-curated semantic nodes and edges such as `motivates`, `tests`, `supports`, `contradicts`, `supersedes`, `blocks`, `splits_into`, `merges_with`.\n\nProvider outputs should attach to both layers: automatically to provenance; optionally to curated project nodes as evidence or planned work.\n\n## Feedbax Integration Sketch\n\nA Feedbax provider would declare capabilities for:\n\n- Opening a project/run/spec in Feedbax Studio.\n- Validating graph/model/task/loss/training/evaluation/analysis specs.\n- Starting training/evaluation/analysis jobs.\n- Publishing manifests for run sets, runs, evals, analyses, and reports.\n- Registering local components or analysis nodes generated under Mandible review.\n\nFeedbax-side companion issue: `f68cf66` in the Feedbax repo.\n\n## MVP Plan\n\n1. Add Mandible artifact-store schema and local content-addressed storage backend.\n2. Add provider registry table/config and a static provider manifest format.\n3. Add provider capability discovery endpoint/API.\n4. Add artifact ingest API returning stable artifact IDs.\n5. Add manifest ingest API that creates graph/provenance nodes and edges.\n6. Add UI action registry so graph nodes can show provider actions conditionally.\n7. Wire a first provider: Feedbax, initially for open/validate/import/export before remote execution.\n8. Let Mandible agents query provider schemas and validate specs through provider tools.\n\n## Design Questions\n\n- Is the provider registry local-config only at first, or should providers self-register via HTTP/CLI/MCP?\n- Should artifact IDs be purely content-addressed, or include logical project namespaces for human navigation?\n- How should review/auth policies apply to provider actions that launch compute or mutate local component registries?\n- How are provider schemas versioned and migrated when old manifests remain in the graph?\n- How does Mandible represent planned specs that have not yet been executed?\n- How should provider-specific graph nodes be displayed without hardcoding every provider in Mandible UI code?\n\n## Related\n\n- `0516fc0` — Mandible design coordination.\n- `cf51a72` — specialized visual representation for node/edge types in knowledge graphs.\n- `b353fff` — DAG/graph-based workflow representation for presets.\n- `84cbfcc` — shared graph rendering infrastructure.\n- `3d85f43` — project registry canonical name-to-path resolution.\n- `f3dbb3e` — inbox/work-queue dispatch model.\n- Feedbax `f68cf66` — provider contract for Feedbax specs, components, runs, and artifacts.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}